<Base title="About llmcpu" description="How the language-model CPU works, what the knobs do, and where the idea came from.">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p>
      A processor is a machine that fetches a few bytes, decides what they mean, does that, and moves on. This site
      replaces the part that decides with a language model. Everything else, memory, registers, the program counter, the
      console, is ordinary and exact. The model is handed the bytes at the program counter and a small set of tools:
      read memory, write memory, read a register, write a register, do one piece of 32-bit arithmetic, and move the
      program counter. Moving the program counter is what finishes an instruction.
    </p>
    <p>
      A silicon processor has one fixed idea of what bytes mean, and stops dead at the first word that does not fit it.
      The model has no such idea unless you give it one. By default it is told only that the bytes are a program in a
      language it alone knows: read as many as make an instruction, decide what it means, act, be consistent. So a poem
      is a program. So is a photograph, or noise. The question the site asks is not whether the model gets it right,
      because there is no right, but what it makes of what it is given: how it chunks the bytes, what it decides they
      do, and what the program turns out to print.
    </p>
    <p>
      Real RISC-V programs are in the gallery too, as one kind of input among the others. For those you can ask for a
      second, silicon processor to run the same bytes alongside, and see exactly where the model's reading parts from
      the official one. That comparison is a good way to learn what a processor actually does, but it is the second
      act, not the point.
    </p>

    <h2>The machine</h2>
    <p>
      It is a 32-bit RISC-V machine with the base integer instruction set only (RV32I, about forty instructions) and
      16 KiB of memory. Storing a word to address <code>0x4000</code> prints its low byte; storing to
      <code>0x4100</code> halts. The curated programs are small C programs compiled with clang for exactly this
      machine, with no standard library; their sources, binaries and line tables are in the
      <a href="https://github.com/ANUcybernetics/llmcpu/tree/main/programs">programs directory</a>. The colours running
      through the source, assembly, memory and trace panes follow the compiler's own line table, the same trick
      <a href="https://godbolt.org">Compiler Explorer</a> uses to show which line became which instructions.
    </p>

    <h2>The model</h2>
    <p>
      Models run entirely in your browser through <a href="https://github.com/mlc-ai/web-llm">WebLLM</a> on WebGPU.
      Nothing you load, paste or drop leaves your machine. The dropdown lists every small Qwen the runtime ships
      prebuilt, plus a couple of other families for comparison; the first load downloads the weights, which the browser
      then caches. The model's replies are constrained to a JSON grammar, so it physically cannot produce a malformed
      tool call, only a wrong one. Every reply also carries a one-sentence comment, which is the narration you see in
      the trace.
    </p>
    <p>
      The perfect oracle in the same dropdown is not a language model. It is a silicon RISC-V processor answering in the
      model's place: it reads RISC-V and nothing else, so on a poem it shrugs and steps over every four bytes. It exists
      so that the machinery can be seen working on a device without WebGPU.
    </p>

    <h2>The knobs</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>How to read the bytes</strong> is the main one. The default tells the model nothing about what an
        instruction is: it chooses how many bytes to take and what they mean. The other three settings tell it the bytes
        are RISC-V, with increasing help: raw words only, a compact manual, or the manual plus each instruction already
        decoded for it. They form a ladder from invention to execution.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>State shown each step</strong> sets how much of the machine the model sees without asking: just the
        program counter and its four bytes, or the non-zero registers too, or nearby memory and the console as well. The
        rest it has to fetch with tools.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Thinking before acting</strong> gives the model a scratchpad of a few sentences or a paragraph before it
        commits to its tool calls.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Instructions remembered</strong> is how many of its own recent steps the model is reminded of. It is the
        model's only memory of what happened before; the machine's state is the rest.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Prior art</h2>
    <p>
      In 2023 Brad Grantham had GPT-4 <a href="https://bradgrantham.github.io/gpt-4-6502/">single-step a 6502 FizzBuzz</a>,
      one instruction per prompt with a line of reasoning before each state update; it got 286 of 288 steps right. The
      idea of a learned system standing in for a deterministic one has since shown up at other layers: diffusion models
      that <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.14837">render a game engine</a>, a model that
      <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.08800">predicts an operating system's screen</a>, and benchmarks measuring how
      well language models
      <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.14777">execute a state machine over long horizons</a> (badly, unless the state
      is externalised at every step, which is what the tools here do). What we have not seen is a control unit asked to read bytes
      that were never a program as though they were one, with the reading laid out beside the bytes.
    </p>

